            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Measures the size of a tensor: by default its length, the square root of the sum of the squares. Given a dim, it measures along that dim only - so t.linalg.norm -1 on 24 velocity vectors (24 x 3) gives the 24 speeds, one per joint.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.norm <dim>\nWithout a dim, the whole tensor gives one number. With one, a dim box and a keep_dims\ncheckbox appear.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to measure. Every input sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim measured along: -1, the last, measures each row.\n\nkeep_dims (checkbox, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nKeep the measured dim, as size 1, so the answer lines up with the input (to divide by it, for instance, making unit vectors).\n\nord:\nAn option, under the node. 2 (the default) is ordinary length; 1 is the sum of the sizes (distance walking a grid); inf is the largest size; 0 counts the non-zero values.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nnorm:\nThe length(s).\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: the whole tensor 3 4 is one vector, length 5. Bottom: with dim -1, each row is its own vector: 3 4 is 5, 6 8 is 10, 5 12 is 13.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.norm is the numpy version. t.length gives the length of a single vector;\nt.cdist the distances between points; t.normalize makes vectors length 1.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1050,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Measures the size of a tensor: by default its length, the square root of the sum of the squares. Given a dim, it measures along that dim only - so t.linalg.norm -1 on 24 velocity vectors (24 x 3) gives the 24 speeds, one per joint.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.norm <dim>\nWithout a dim, the whole tensor gives one number. With one, a dim box and a keep_dims\ncheckbox appear.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to measure. Every input sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim measured along: -1, the last, measures each row.\n\nkeep_dims (checkbox, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nKeep the measured dim, as size 1, so the answer lines up with the input (to divide by it, for instance, making unit vectors).\n\nord:\nAn option, under the node. 2 (the default) is ordinary length; 1 is the sum of the sizes (distance walking a grid); inf is the largest size; 0 counts the non-zero values.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nnorm:\nThe length(s).\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: the whole tensor 3 4 is one vector, length 5. Bottom: with dim -1, each row is its own vector: 3 4 is 5, 6 8 is 10, 5 12 is 13.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.norm is the numpy version. t.length gives the length of a single vector;\nt.cdist the distances between points; t.normalize makes vectors length 1."
        },
